Z||000010||EdsB52||06-01-2001||08:27 PM||||Maybe you can decode that last post for us. Anyway, no, I'm not blowing off on you, just trying to help you realize a little reality. First of all, you didn't tell us if you have a 5sp or automatic. With the 5 speed, the 4 cyl is a real corner burner. I would blow away puss-stains (mustangs) and Camaros through the corners, to have them smoke by me in the straights. With the 6 banger, I feel the weight and know that I would have my hands full if it got squirly, whereas the I could change my line while in the corner with the 4. While driving my old 4 banger, I came together in a corner with an old RX7 at 80mph without incident. Pick your poison, there is no perfect machine for all conditions. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Indiana_resto_guy||06-01-2001||08:43 PM||||If your car and the friends GT are automatics, they're not much for performance. They are good transportation.<P>However if you have and or your friend has a manual transmission, both engines are tired or just not running correctly.<BR>How many miles are on your car and how much history were you able to obtain?<BR>A HUGE problem with many Fieros is that they were not properly maintained in the past. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||maryjane||06-01-2001||10:19 PM||||It is all about what  you want out of the Fiero. Yeah, we would all like the hp and torque of a hemi or 427 in a little Pontiac and there are some out there that can cram you back in the seat. But they have had thousands of $ and as many hours spent on them to make em like that. Most people don't own a Fiero just for the speed, it is the way it feels in your hands,thru the wheel and the seat of your pants. It's like the other reply said; take it out on a twisty road and let it go. And RELAX a little and FEEL the car and road. Then get out and look at it. They are right, it's not a muscle car( with a few exceptions), but it's a good, solid ride with a lot to give, and there is a lot you can do to em to make yours unique<BR>They aren't for everyone,but they are special cars. Mine is a '84 4cyl auto- as stock as it came from Pontiac. I've owned a vette, it had lot of power, but didn't hug a curve with as much feel and excitment as the Fiero.<BR>Try that and if it doesn't trip somethin in you, sell it and find something you do like.<BR>There's always someone here lookin for one. ||||reg||6
Z||000013||Galen||06-01-2001||11:21 PM||||Do I smell something? here, let me take that shovel, and let you rest for a bit. hit 50 from stop light to stop light, how close are the stop lights? my auto seems to shift out of first at around 35.. of course, my tranny is bad so it shifts erractic.I can hit 75 from the crossroads down the road from my house to my house, maybe a half a mile..thats not driving it hard.. I dunno.. hre you can have your shovel back now<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe Auto 4Banger - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR>AIM: WookieeNC||||reg||1
Z||000014||StuGood||06-02-2001||12:32 AM||||Man-!  Sounds like classic "buyer's remorse," doesn't it?<P>Well, the best thing to do now, is look at the positives.  Since your car is a 1987 model, you've got the larger fuel tank; since it's a Duke, it has the potential to get decent mileage.  If it's an automatic, be glad you won't be having to replace a clutch, nor will you have to mess with bleeding the bleeding thing.  Less hassle, and no clutch to wear out in heavy, stop-and-go traffic. <P>I owned three V6 Fieros, before I bought a Duke-powered one.  The V6's are great, and I'll always like them best, because I love their performance.  But I've been driving my little Duke-powered one for the last couple of weeks, and you know what?  It's really growing on me!<P>It's surprising what a person gets used to.  A long time ago, in Seattle, I had a Beretta - 2.0 liter automatic, loaded.  I talked myself into buying the car because it was going to be my only car, and it had to be reliable, with good resale value.  At first I kicked myself for buying such a wimpy car - I missed my Fiero!  But you know, after a few weeks getting used to driving it, I actually got to where I enjoyed driving it.  Soon, I was used to it, and after that I never really missed the Fiero much. <P>So, just give it some time, and quit kicking yourself.  There's no reason to, and it won't change anything for the better.  The money is spent.  It's up to you, now, to enjoy what you have  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||Iron Duke Wellingsley||06-02-2001||12:38 AM||||It is not easy to reply positively to this kind of thread.  <P>It seems that we have some misplaced expectations.  It could be one of the following:<P>1.  You bought a used Fiero for 1 to $2K and you expect it to run like a concours show winner<P>2.  You have the reflexes and ankle strength of a dead squirrel<P>3.  You are running a 50/50 water/gas fuel mixture<P>4.  The car you bought has zero compression in all four cylinders<P>5.  The transmission bands are worn to the point of failure<P>6.  There's no air in the tires<P>7.  You decided to remove the coolant to save weight<P>8.  The oxygen sensor is dust and the car has fried the catalytic converter<P>9.  That "mystery-magic sand" product you bought at the flea market didn't improve gas mileage when you added it to the crankcase<P>10.  Root beer is not the ultimate fuel additive after all<P>I have driven a driven a B210 and any healthy Fiero can beat one to 60.  You should congratulate your Mom, she clearly can drive circles around you!<P>This whole thread just goes to show "It's not the car, it's the driver . . ."<p>[This message has been edited by Iron Duke Wellingsley (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000016||Galen||06-02-2001||12:52 AM||||Iron Duke, I'd say you hit the nail on the head<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe Auto 4Banger - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR>AIM: WookieeNC||||reg||1
Z||000017||EdsB52||06-02-2001||12:34 PM||||I agree! When I drove my 86 5speed, I didn't have a problem once I got it up to 80 or 90, the acceleration was a little weak, but tollerable. But once at 90, it only tached 3000, and would do it all day. I drove from Phoenix to Las Vegas (300 miles) in under 4 hours one time at 3am. That trip usually takes most people 6-8 hours. The road is windey and is nick-named "blood-highway." It has little white crosses posted along the side where people have eaten it. Sometimes the crosses are in groups of 7 or 8. I would smoke through there at 80-90 mph, even in the windey stuff. Ya, the 4 banger is a little sad on acceleration, but any butt-fnck can drive fast in a straight line. Like one previous poster wrote, what do you expect for $1,500? If you want handling and power, you'll spend a minimum of $5,000, and that car is probably thrashed, as it cost closer to $25,000 when new. Why don't you sell your car to someone who might appreciate it.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Philphine||06-02-2001||01:21 PM||||actually lifeless, i've always felt the same about my 2.5. it's reliable at best but otherwise i hate it. the saving grace for me is that i also have a v8 car. i have to assume you drove it before you got it (or was it something you brought and fixed up? i can't see a 4cyl $1500 fixer upper.) so you should have known before you paid for it.<P>that being said, i love my car. enough so that now i have a few of them. i'd say do all the things you can to it (suspension mods, styling, stereo type stuff) and work toward an engine change like i am. read some of the engine swap threads for ideas (the bubbajoe v8 is in the lead with me now. got some of his mounts), find a direction to go in and work toward it. <P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)<BR>'84 chop top (parts car or project. time will tell)||||reg||1
Z||000019||Necromancer||06-02-2001||10:18 PM||||You know, people rag on the the ol' 4 cyl Duke, but I kinda like it. Our '86 is a ton of fun to whip around in, I can wind her up and run with any 4 or 6 out there. (definately quicker than my 4liter v6 ranger, and it whups #1 sons v6 camaro).  That said, you also need to remember that the fiero's strength is *not* as a straight line drag racer.  You have to take it for a ride on a nice twisty road to apprecaite the car.  It sticks like glue.  Drop down a gear, wind that duke up to 3-4k and whip around some corners with the sunroof out, windows down and the stereo up.  Mmmmmmmmmmmmm<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||camaro2fiero||06-04-2001||01:27 AM||||What The !! I spent 2900.00 on my 84 2m4 with not many options,sunroof, tilt and air, soft ray glass,3 speed auto,radio with the cassette,luggage and trunk compartment light.33000 miles,aluminum wheels.The trunk area has never even had the jack out since new and still has the retainers from the factory. My friend I bought it off of spent 600 on new struts and shocks.he did this because he wanted to upgrade this since it has been 16 years of sitting on the originals.I have been told that 2900, might have been a little high.But when I looked around town for a low mileage car in nice condition.5000 at least and then the mileage wasnt that low.This was the best car purchase in my opinion.This little 4 banger turns heads.Can definetly do 50 MPH between lights.Cornering has been thrilling to say the least.Fiero definetly holds its own even 17 years later when it comes to design and driving satisfaction.I get decent gas mileage.Now I want another one.Nuff said You should go separate ways from your Fiero if the only thing you want is straight line speed.||||reg||1
Z||000021||84Bill||06-04-2001||01:49 AM||||Ahem..... The Fiero is NOT a "sports car" It was never designed to be a fast "Sports car" It was designed to be a basic commuter car kinda like a toyota carolla but with a sporty look and unique engine arrangement. You can make it into a fast car much like you can make a corolla into a fast car. BUT a Fiero weighs more than a corolla BUT a corolla wont handel a corner as well as a Fiero can. So as far as a "Fast Fiero" is concerned  you can only compair it to cars that were designed with the same idea in mind. <BR>Kinda like comparing apples to apples and such not apples to oranges. Not only that but the car is old I mean 84 to 88! try running against any car of that era, hummmm interesting concept huh. I'll be willing to bet you can whip the pants off that corolla!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Any questions?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||6
